Closes #36.

Small Qwen3 models (0.6B, 1.7B) on ANE get stuck in unclosed `<think>` blocks and never produce a real response within reasonable token budgets — matches the "uncontrolled `<think>` tags" + "repetition" symptoms reported in #36.

Qwen3's HF chat template already supports `enable_thinking` in its Jinja context — when false it emits a pre-closed `<think>\n\n</think> \n\n` block before the assistant's first output so the model skips thinking-mode. AnemllCore's `applyChatTemplate` previously called the swift-transformers overload that doesn't forward template context, so callers couldn't reach the flag.

This adds `enableThinking: Bool = true` to the public `applyChatTemplate` surface. When false, forwards as `additionalContext: ["enable_thinking": false]` — the HF Jinja template does the rest. Default `true` preserves all existing caller behavior (no `additionalContext` passed, no change in output).

Scope:
- Primary path (swift-transformers Jinja render) — kwarg-forwarding, no per-family hardcoding. No-op for any model whose template doesn't read `enable_thinking` (Jinja `is defined and is false` short-circuits).
- Fallback path (manual ChatML string construction, when Jinja throws) — inline `<think>\n\n</think>\n\n` append for the "qwen", "qwen2", "qwen3" cases only.

Verified end-to-end on M4 Pro ANE via a downstream Swift + MLX chat harness (Dynamic Memory Controller, to be open-sourced separately):

Before the fix:
- Qwen3-0.6B: 1024-token budget filled with `\n—\n—\n—` loops inside an unclosed `<think>` block
- Qwen3-1.7B: 10+ repetitions of the same phrase inside unclosed `<think>`
- Either model: no response prose emitted within reasonable budget

After the fix (same models, same prompt):
- Qwen3-0.6B: stops at EOS at 62 tokens, 5 clean factual sentences, 57 tok/s
- Qwen3-1.7B: produces response prose classifiable by the downstream parser, 44 tok/s

